## SonarQube

`sonar-project.properties` sets the project key `{{.Opt.Name}}`, sources, test files and the
Go coverage report (`coverage.out`). Create a project with that key in SonarQube and a token for it.
{{- if or (.Opt.HasExtra "github-actions") (.Opt.HasExtra "gitlab-ci")}}

CI runs the scan after the tests once these are set (the step is skipped while `SONAR_TOKEN` is missing):
{{if .Opt.HasExtra "github-actions"}}
- GitHub: repository secret `SONAR_TOKEN`, and `SONAR_HOST_URL` as a repository variable (or secret).
{{- end}}
{{- if .Opt.HasExtra "gitlab-ci"}}
- GitLab: CI/CD variables `SONAR_HOST_URL` and `SONAR_TOKEN` (masked, protected if only `main` scans).
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

Never commit the token: put it in CI secrets or your shell only.

Scan from your machine (needs Docker):

```bash
go test -coverprofile=coverage.out ./...
export SONAR_HOST_URL=https://sonarqube.example.com SONAR_TOKEN=...   # your token, not committed
docker run --rm -e SONAR_HOST_URL -e SONAR_TOKEN -v "$PWD:/usr/src" {{.SonarImage}}
```
{{- end}}
{{- if .Migrations}}

## Migrations

SQL files in `migrations/` (`<version>_<name>.up.sql` / `.down.sql`) are embedded into the binary
and applied with golang-migrate when the service starts. Versions run in numeric order and
applied ones are recorded in the `schema_migrations` table.
`projgen add entity` writes its table's migration here, so it runs on the next start.

Test them against a throwaway database:

```bash
TEST_DATABASE_URL=... go test -run 'Migrate|Repository' ./...
```
{{- end}}

## Logging

Logs are JSON with snake_case fields: `@timestamp`, `level`, `message`, `service`, `env`, `request_id`, `logger`.
Keep `message` constant and put dynamic values in their own fields:

```go
logger.FromContext(ctx, log).Info("order created", "order_id", id)
```

Never log passwords, tokens, API keys or personal data.
